From 6ec1c4866a69725e8bff1409514a465bef7ff245 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Horizon authors <>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 18:31:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Choose the frames a tick shows from what the last ticks cost

The frames a tick shows were all or nothing: three passes (60) or one (20),
chosen at the title by a CPU probe. The probe cannot see what a core really
presents (a fast mobile core with ordinary polling speed got 20 fps where it
shows 54), and three passes in a scene that cannot carry them give 20 fps
with uneven spacing (a tournament at the original speed: every first pass
late).

A ladder of three cadences now decides from the real workload, in every loop
that goes through modPresent: 60 (three passes, a pass that does not fit
skipped as before), steady 30 (the curling pair of patch 0292 for every scene:
one frame at 2/3 of an even tick that ends a refresh early, two at 1/3 and
3/3 of the odd tick computed in that spare refresh, every frame two refreshes
apart) and 20 (one frame at 3/3, the original's timing). modCadArm arms the
level at the start of a tick (MOD_RENDER_CADENCE 8 | half for the pair, 16
for one frame; curling's own 1/2/4 are left alone and only they flush its
tiles). modCadRecord stores each tick's cost (tick + first pass and the extra
pass, in lines) and outcome (frames planned, shown, late) in a 32-tick ring
and decides: down from 60 after 16 ticks that show fewer than
MOD_CAD_KEEP60/32 frames a tick (to steady 30 when every tick fits two
refreshes, else to 20); down from 30 when more than MOD_CAD_KEEP30 frames per
32 ticks are late or skipped; up only after a full window whose costs predict
the higher cadence, and a wait that doubles after every failed try (32 to
1024 ticks). The world and the battle loops remember their levels; a map
change clears the evidence and the wait but keeps the level. Fast battle
ticks, pumped loads, pause frames and stalled ticks are not recorded. The
pass budget checks stay, so no cadence slows the 20 Hz logic.

MOD_FPS_POLICY: 0 the ladder (default), 1/2/3 lock 60 / steady 30 / 20, 4 the
CPU-probe selection, the only policy in which the title probe and the idle
polls write MOD_FPS_MODE_RUN; it otherwise stays at the build's rate. The
probe still runs for the title notice.
